Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 09:00:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 076/119] scene-referred S3: the conversion node, and the Prev
 copy that must precede it

One 3x3 per texel turns the frame into the working space. In place on $HDRTarget's
UAV, dispatched from ExecuteHDRPostProcessing() between the $HDRTargetPrev copy and
the depth-of-field stage, only when CRendererResources::IsSceneReferred().

Why that exact line. It is the only window in the frame where every writer of
Rec.709 scene colour has finished - deferred and forward opaque, sky, ocean
caustics, volumetric fog, global fog, clouds, water, transparents, half-res
particles, snow displacement and the rain layer - and nothing has read the target
for post yet. One line earlier and the rain layer authors 709 into an AP1 target;
one line later and depth of field has already run on mixed data.

Why in place. Each thread reads and writes only its own texel, so there is no
cross-texel dependency and no race: no scratch target, no copy-back, no pipeline
pointer to retarget (m_pTexHDRTarget is read by about fifteen stages, by per-pass
resource sets and by the ESRAM residency code). The pixel-shader form of the same
point costs two full-screen passes because a render target cannot be its own shader
resource. The UAV flag is already there - CTiledShadingStage writes this texture
through SetOutputUAV - so no plumbing is new. About 0.08 ms at 1080p.
decisions/s3-working-space.md 1B stays the documented fallback if the typed UAV load
of R16G16B16A16_FLOAT ever proves unavailable, and the code comment says so.

Why not at the writers. A 3x3 is linear, so additive and alpha blending commute
with it - anything that blended before this point lands in the same place either
way. Multiplicative and min/max blends do NOT, and GS_BLSRC_DSTCOL / GS_BLDST_SRCCOL
are reachable from material shader scripts. Converting after all scene blending
makes the question moot.

$HDRTargetPrev stays Rec.709 and the copy stays in front of the dispatch, with a
comment at the copy saying why: it is not a post-chain history, it is the texture
SSR, the water reflection and the SVOGI bounce bind NEXT frame and consume inside
the 709 lighting pass. AP1 numbers read as 709 give desaturated, hue-shifted
reflections and bounce with no error anywhere, and SSR's limiter is a per-channel
min, which is not basis-invariant.

That leaves exactly one post-conversion consumer of Prev, and it was a real bug:
motion blur reads $SceneTargetR11G11B10F[0] when depth of field ran and Prev
otherwise. The first is ACEScg (copied from the converted target), the second is
Rec.709 - so with DOF off the stock path would composite a 709 history over an AP1
frame. The copy is now forced under the switch, and the same flag drives the dirty
key and the texture selection so the three cannot drift apart. Cost: one full-res
copy in the DOF-off case.

r_SceneReferredDebug (VF_NULL, default 0):
  1 = skip the conversion, everything else scene-referred stays on. The A/B that
      separates "the primaries moved" from every other thing this pipeline changed.
  2 = paint what the conversion could not produce - RED for NaN/Inf, GREEN for a
      negative channel, at 64x so a marked texel is unmistakable. Both should be
      impossible: Rec.709's primaries lie inside AP1, so the forward matrix is
      entirely non-negative. An empty frame is therefore the S3 acceptance test.
      A marked texel means the frame was already broken upstream, or the matrix is
      not being applied to what we think it is.

Mode 2 lives in the conversion shader itself, at the conversion point, which leaves
r_HDRDebug 3 (HDRFinalSceneDebugPS, %_RT_DEBUG1) untouched and doing the same test
at the END of the chain, after DOF, the glare family and motion blur. Two probes,
two questions, and the HWSR_DEBUG axis is not disturbed.

RenderDoc marker: SCENE_REFERRED_CONVERT_709_TO_AP1.

